window.ACADEMY_DATA = window.ACADEMY_DATA || {quizzes:{}, flashcards:{}, exams:{}};

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.quizzes, {
"astronomy:1": [
{"q":"The Greek word planetes means \u201cwanderers.\u201d Why were Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter, and Saturn grouped together under that name?","choices":["They are the only stars visible without a telescope","Unlike the fixed stars, they move through the constellations over days and weeks","They are physically larger than every other star","They never change brightness"],"answer":1,"explain":"Most stars hold fixed patterns, but these five drift against the background over time \u2014 the visible signature of planets orbiting the sun. \u201cWanderer\u201d was a description of observed motion, not size or brightness, and it is why we still call them planets."},
{"q":"The sun and moon always travel along the ecliptic. What does this tell you about where to look for the zodiac constellations?","choices":["They are scattered all over the sky","They lie only in the band along the ecliptic, never far from it","They cluster around the north celestial pole","They appear only directly overhead at midnight"],"answer":1,"explain":"The zodiac is defined as the twelve constellations along the sun and moon\u2019s highway \u2014 so they are confined to that band. This is also why planets are always found near the ecliptic: everything in the solar system travels roughly the same road."},
{"q":"You find the Big Dipper at 9 PM, then look again at 3 AM \u2014 six hours later. Roughly how far has the Dipper wheeled around Polaris?","choices":["Barely at all \u2014 stars move too slowly to notice in one night","About a quarter turn","About halfway around","A full circle"],"answer":1,"explain":"Earth rotates 360\u00b0 in 24 hours, so in 6 hours the sky wheels about 90\u00b0 \u2014 a quarter turn around the nearly-fixed Polaris. The lesson\u2019s two-hour check shows the same motion on a smaller scale: this is Earth\u2019s rotation observed directly."}
],
"astronomy:2": [
{"q":"What observed problem were Ptolemy\u2019s epicycles designed to solve?","choices":["Why stars twinkle near the horizon","Retrograde motion \u2014 planets sometimes looping backward before resuming course","Why Polaris barely moves","The phases of Venus"],"answer":1,"explain":"Planets don\u2019t just drift forward; they periodically loop backward. Ptolemy\u2019s stacked circles \u2014 each planet on a small circle (epicycle) whose center rides a big circle (deferent) around Earth \u2014 reproduce those loops geometrically, as you can feel by walking a big circle while swinging your arm in a small one."},
{"q":"The lesson insists Ptolemy was not simply \u201cwrong.\u201d What is the deeper point?","choices":["His model made no testable predictions at all","It predicted planetary positions well enough to navigate by for 1,400 years despite being structurally false \u2014 predictive success is not the same as truth","It was immediately falsified by his contemporaries","It was a religious doctrine, not a scientific model"],"answer":1,"explain":"A model can be useful without being true. Ptolemy\u2019s Earth-centered machinery was wrong about structure yet excellent at prediction, which is why science demands more than successful forecasts: it demands the right underlying mechanism."},
{"q":"A modern researcher keeps adding adjustable parameters to a model until it fits every data point perfectly. What caution from the Ptolemy lesson applies?","choices":["More parameters always means a truer model","With enough stacked circles you can reproduce any observed path \u2014 fitting data is not the same as discovering truth","Old models should never be questioned","Prediction is worthless in science"],"answer":1,"explain":"Epicycles show that sheer complexity can mimic any observation without approaching reality. A model that fits everything by construction explains nothing \u2014 the Ptolemaic trap is overfitting, ancient edition, and the cure is the same: prefer the model with the right mechanism, not the most knobs."}
],
"astronomy:3": [
{"q":"In the Copernican system, why does retrograde motion \u201cfall out naturally\u201d instead of needing epicycles?","choices":["Because orbits became elliptical","Because telescopes proved Earth moves","Because a faster inner planet (Earth) laps a slower outer one (Mars), making the outer planet appear to move backward \u2014 like a faster car seeming to slide back relative to a slower one","Because the sun\u2019s gravity pulls unevenly"],"answer":2,"explain":"Retrograde is a perspective effect of relative motion, not a real reversal. Once Earth is placed inside Mars\u2019s orbit and moving faster, the apparent backward loop is exactly what you\u2019d expect \u2014 no extra circles required. Simplicity, not new data, was Copernicus\u2019s first argument."},
{"q":"A planet is observed moving fastest at one point in its orbit and slowest at the opposite point. Where is it when fastest, and which law says so?","choices":["Farthest from the sun \u2014 the law of ellipses","Nearest the sun \u2014 the equal-areas law","Nearest the sun \u2014 the harmony law","At a focus \u2014 the law of ellipses"],"answer":1,"explain":"Kepler\u2019s second law: the sun-to-planet line sweeps equal areas in equal times. When the line is short (near the sun), the planet must cover more orbital distance to sweep the same area \u2014 so it moves faster. Newton later explained why: gravity."},
{"q":"In the string-and-pins ellipse drawing, you move the pins farther apart and the ellipse stretches. What do the pins represent in a real orbit?","choices":["The planet at two different times","The two foci of the ellipse \u2014 the sun sits at one of them","The start and end of the orbital year","The nearest and farthest stars"],"answer":1,"explain":"An ellipse is defined by its two foci: every point on the curve keeps the sum of its distances to the pins constant. Kepler\u2019s first law puts the sun at one focus \u2014 which is precisely why orbits are off-center and planets speed up and slow down."}
],
"astronomy:4": [
{"q":"Why did Eratosthenes need two locations (Syene and Alexandria) instead of just one?","choices":["To average out measurement errors between two wells","One shadow angle alone is meaningless \u2014 the difference (7.2\u00b0) between the two is the fraction of Earth\u2019s full circle lying between the cities","Because one well wasn\u2019t deep enough to see the sun","To check whether the weather differed"],"answer":1,"explain":"A single shadow angle tells you nothing about the planet\u2019s size. Two locations give the difference in angle, and that difference is a slice of 360\u00b0 \u2014 scale the inter-city distance up by that fraction and you get the whole circumference. Difference, not measurement, unlocks the scale."},
{"q":"Eratosthenes got about 39,000 km versus the true 40,075 km. What is the lesson\u2019s takeaway?","choices":["Ancient measurements are too crude to trust","A stick, a well, and geometry got within a few percent \u2014 careful reasoning beats fancy instruments","He was simply lucky","The method only works at noon on the solstice"],"answer":1,"explain":"The point is methodological, not technological: with clear geometry and two good observations, a stick outperformed guesswork by orders of magnitude. Precision comes from the reasoning chain, not the equipment budget."},
{"q":"In the basketball-sun scale model, Earth is a peppercorn 26 meters away and Neptune a marble nearly a kilometer off. A friend says the solar system is densely packed. What does the model actually show?","choices":["The planets are evenly spaced beads on a string","The solar system is mostly nothing \u2014 and the emptiness is what the universe is made of","The model proves distances don\u2019t matter","Proxima Centauri is just past Neptune"],"answer":1,"explain":"Shrink the sun to a basketball and the planets become specks separated by football fields of void \u2014 and Proxima Centauri, the nearest star, would be another basketball in Hawaii. Physical scale models defeat the intuition that space is \u201cstuff with gaps\u201d; it is gaps with occasional stuff."}
],
"astronomy:5": [
{"q":"Why is redshift-proportional-to-distance strong evidence that space itself is expanding, rather than just galaxies flying apart through static space?","choices":["Because redshift only happens in an expanding universe","Because the stretch grows with distance in every direction \u2014 exactly what you\u2019d see if space were expanding and carrying galaxies along, like raisins in rising dough","Because nearby galaxies show no redshift at all","Because the lesson says Doppler measurements are unreliable"],"answer":1,"explain":"It\u2019s the pattern that convinces: every distant galaxy\u2019s light is stretched by an amount proportional to its distance, in all directions. That uniformity is the signature of space itself stretching \u2014 carrying galaxies with it \u2014 rather than objects merely moving through a fixed stage."},
{"q":"The lesson calls dark matter and dark energy \u201cplaceholders for observed effects.\u201d What does that mean?","choices":["They are proven particles awaiting names","They are names for things we observe indirectly whose actual nature is unknown \u2014 honesty markers, not explanations","They are fictional concepts cosmologists invented to get funding","They are the cause of the Big Bang"],"answer":1,"explain":"We see gravitational and expansion effects that visible matter can\u2019t account for, so we label the unknowns rather than pretending we understand them. The lesson presents this as a virtue: Socrates\u2019s \u2018I know that I know nothing\u2019 is still good scientific practice."},
{"q":"Which of the following is NOT among the lesson\u2019s three lines of evidence for the Big Bang?","choices":["Redshift proportional to distance","The cosmic microwave background \u2014 the cooled afterglow of the hot early state","Observed abundances of light elements matching Big Bang predictions","Galaxy rotation curves"],"answer":3,"explain":"The lesson\u2019s three evidences are redshift, the microwave background measurable in every direction, and light-element abundances. Galaxy rotation curves are evidence for dark matter \u2014 a real observation, but a different argument. Keeping which evidence supports which claim is the heart of honest cosmology."}
],
"astronomy:6": [
{"q":"The capstone pairs stargazing with a Marcus Aurelius\u2013style journal: zoom out to cosmic scale, then zoom back in. What is the exercise training?","choices":["Memorizing constellation names under pressure","Distinguishing real problems from imagined ones \u2014 testing which worries survive contact with a larger scale","Calculating stellar distances from memory","Proving that human concerns are meaningless"],"answer":1,"explain":"Aurelius wrote that many disturbances \u2018lie entirely in your imagination.\u2019 The zoom-out doesn\u2019t declare everything meaningless; it filters \u2014 what still matters at cosmic scale is what genuinely matters, and the rest can be set down. That\u2019s the inward half of the Greek pairing: observation outward, examination inward."},
{"q":"The capstone says apps may confirm your constellations but must not replace finding them yourself. Which skill is being protected?","choices":["Smartphone proficiency","Naked-eye orientation \u2014 reading the sky as a map from pattern and memory, the lesson-1 skill","Astrophotography technique","Latin star names"],"answer":1,"explain":"The point of finding the Dipper, Cassiopeia, Orion, Leo, and Scorpius yourself is the ancient competence: one anchor star (Polaris) turning chaos into a map. An app that does the finding for you skips the very faculty the course is building."},
{"q":"\u201cThe Greeks always paired them\u201d \u2014 outward and inward. What does that pairing claim about studying the cosmos?","choices":["Astronomy and psychology are the same science","Looking at the universe and examining the self belong together \u2014 each corrects the other","Telescopes are unnecessary","Mythology outranks observation"],"answer":1,"explain":"The capstone\u2019s two tasks \u2014 find five constellations, then journal in the style of Marcus Aurelius \u2014 enact the Greek conviction that cosmic perspective should reshape the inner life. Astronomy without self-examination is trivia; self-examination without a larger scale is myopia."}
]
});

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.quizzes, {
"philosophy:1": [
{"q":"After a market crash wipes out his savings, a man\u2019s character, friendships, and judgment remain intact. Which school would say his life can still be fully good \u2014 and why?","choices":["The Epicureans \u2014 because pleasure is gone but tranquility remains","The Stoics \u2014 because virtue is the only good fortune can\u2019t take","The Skeptics \u2014 because money was never real","Aristotle \u2014 because wealth was never needed for flourishing"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Stoics hold that virtue \u2014 excellence of character \u2014 is the only true good, precisely because externals like money are hostage to fortune. Note the contrast: Aristotle\u2019s flourishing actually does need some external goods, so he could not say misfortune leaves the good life untouched."},
{"q":"A woman feels cheerful while neglecting her health, her friendships, and her work. What follows from the lesson\u2019s account of eudaimonia?","choices":["She is flourishing, because happiness is the good life","She may feel happy but is not flourishing \u2014 flourishing is the objective condition of a life going well, like health","Feelings are the only valid measure of a life","Flourishing is impossible in modern life"],"answer":1,"explain":"\u2018Flourishing\u2019 beats \u2018happiness\u2019 as a translation because happiness names a feeling \u2014 which can rest on illusion \u2014 while flourishing names a condition assessable from outside. You can feel fine while your life goes badly; you cannot flourish while it does."},
{"q":"Which school would say: \u201cStop arguing about what happens after death \u2014 suspend judgment on what you can\u2019t know, and peace will follow\u201d?","choices":["The Stoics","The Epicureans","The Skeptics","Aristotle\u2019s school"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Skeptics\u2019 distinctive prescription is suspension of judgment: tranquility follows not from having the answers but from releasing the demand for them. The Epicureans also value tranquility, but they reach it through a positive doctrine about pleasure and fear \u2014 not through suspending judgment."}
],
"philosophy:2": [
{"q":"After an hour of questioning, a debater admits: \u201cMy definition of justice collapses \u2014 I don\u2019t actually know what it is.\u201d By Socratic standards, this admission is\u2026","choices":["A humiliating failure","Proof that philosophy is useless","The beginning of wisdom \u2014 recognizing ignorance is exactly what made Socrates the wisest in Athens","A reason to stop asking questions"],"answer":2,"explain":"Socrates\u2019s sole claim to wisdom was awareness of his own ignorance, set against interlocutors who falsely believed they knew. The collapse of false confidence isn\u2019t defeat \u2014 it\u2019s the elenchus working, clearing ground where real inquiry can start."},
{"q":"A friend declares, \u201cFreedom means doing whatever you want.\u201d What is the correct first elenctic move?","choices":["Agree \u2014 it sounds right","Ask what they mean by \u2018free\u2019 and \u2018want\u2019 \u2014 pin down the terms before accepting the claim","Present statistics about freedom","Change the subject to something easier"],"answer":1,"explain":"The elenchus begins by clarifying meaning: most people don\u2019t know what they think until asked twice. \u2018What do I actually mean by that?\u2019 and \u2018what would change my mind?\u2019 are the two questions the lesson prescribes before adopting any belief."},
{"q":"\u201cThe Dunning-Kruger effect is Socrates with a chart.\u201d What does the comparison claim?","choices":["Socrates invented modern statistics","Both describe people overestimating their competence because they cannot recognize their own ignorance","Dunning-Kruger disproved Socratic method","Both are theories about memory failure"],"answer":1,"explain":"The modern finding \u2014 that the least competent are most confident, because the skill needed to judge competence is the skill they lack \u2014 is Socrates\u2019s discovery in empirical dress: his interlocutors\u2019 problem was never stupidity but unrecognized ignorance."}
],
"philosophy:3": [
{"q":"Insulted publicly, a man feels rage and an urge for revenge \u2014 but answers calmly and fairly. In Plato\u2019s tripartite terms, what just happened?","choices":["The cave allegory","Justice in the soul \u2014 spirit and appetite felt their pull, but reason (the charioteer) kept each part doing its job","The theory of the Forms","Akrasia \u2014 weakness of will"],"answer":1,"explain":"Plato\u2019s soul has reason (charioteer), spirit (noble horse: ambition, anger), and appetite (unruly horse). Justice isn\u2019t the absence of anger or hunger \u2014 it\u2019s each part functioning under reason\u2019s guidance. Akrasia would be the opposite: appetite seizing the reins."},
{"q":"A person spends three hours a day outraged by headlines, convinced they understand the world. Which of these best fits Plato\u2019s \u201cshadow\u201d?","choices":["A viral clip engineered to provoke, standing in for a complex event they\u2019ve never examined firsthand","A careful firsthand conversation with someone affected","A direct scientific measurement","Sunlight on the wall"],"answer":0,"explain":"The cave\u2019s shadows are secondhand projections \u2014 reputation, outrage, status, the news cycle \u2014 mistaken for reality itself. The lesson treats the allegory as a diagnostic tool, not metaphysics: regularly ask \u2018what shadows am I watching right now?\u2019 and then look at the thing itself."},
{"q":"The freed prisoner returns to the cave and is mocked. What point does the lesson draw from this?","choices":["Truth is impossible to communicate","Most people, offered the truth, prefer the comfortable shadows \u2014 philosophy is a painful turn toward the light","Philosophers should never return to ordinary life","The majority is always right"],"answer":1,"explain":"The mockery is the point: seeing things as they are requires a difficult, deliberate turn away from comfortable projections, and people resist it. You don\u2019t need Plato\u2019s metaphysics of the Forms to use this \u2014 the resistance to unwelcome truth is observable daily."}
],
"philosophy:4": [
{"q":"A manager must give critical feedback. Cowardice would be avoiding it; recklessness would be a brutal public takedown. What does the doctrine of the mean prescribe?","choices":["Always be blunt \u2014 honesty is a rule","Honest, private, constructive feedback \u2014 courage calibrated to this situation, which takes phronesis to get right","Never criticize anyone","Follow a script so no judgment is needed"],"answer":1,"explain":"The mean isn\u2019t a midpoint on a dial \u2014 courage between cowardice and recklessness looks different in every situation, which is why Aristotle insists on phronesis (practical wisdom): the judgment to see what this case calls for. Rules can\u2019t cover every case; the virtuous person reads the situation."},
{"q":"Why does the lesson say to make the 7-day habituation practice \u201cembarrassingly small\u201d?","choices":["Small acts are morally superior to large ones","The habit must survive your worst day \u2014 virtue is built by repetition, and an ambitious practice collapses under stress","Aristotle disliked ambition","To impress others with your modesty"],"answer":1,"explain":"Virtue is a habit built like a muscle: by repeated action, not by heroic episodes. A practice sized for your best day dies on your worst day \u2014 and it\u2019s the worst days that do the forming. Start small enough that repetition is inevitable."},
{"q":"Someone has read ten books on courage but never once acted courageously. What would Aristotle say?","choices":["They are now courageous \u2014 knowledge is the virtue","They\u2019ve described the target but built nothing \u2014 virtue exists only in the doing, like a sport or an instrument","Reading is superior to acting","Courage can\u2019t be learned at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"\u2018We become just by doing just acts, brave by doing brave acts.\u2019 Virtue is a habit of action and feeling, not a set of propositions \u2014 lectures can point at the target, but only repeated practice rewires the responses. The books are the map, not the territory."}
],
"philosophy:5": [
{"q":"You\u2019re stuck in traffic and will be late. Which response follows the dichotomy of control?","choices":["Fume \u2014 maybe anger will move the traffic","Accept the traffic as not up to you; act on what is \u2014 drive safely, call ahead, govern your attitude","Pretend being late doesn\u2019t matter at all","Blame the city and stay bitter all day"],"answer":1,"explain":"The dichotomy sorts the situation: traffic is on the \u2018not up to me\u2019 list; your response, your effort, your judgment are on the \u2018up to me\u2019 list. Suffering comes from demanding control over the first list \u2014 serenity (and effectiveness) from working the second."},
{"q":"A Stoic trains hard for a promotion, doesn\u2019t get it, and moves on without bitterness. What does this illustrate about \u201cpreferred indifferents\u201d?","choices":["Apathy \u2014 Stoics don\u2019t care about anything","That effort is pointless","Pursuing preferred things energetically while refusing to be enslaved by the outcome","That Stoics never pursue promotions"],"answer":2,"explain":"Health, wealth, and reputation are preferred (rational to pursue) but indifferent to flourishing \u2014 they can\u2019t make you good or ruin you. The caricature says Stoics don\u2019t care; the doctrine says they care without being owned. Marcus Aurelius ran an empire on exactly this."},
{"q":"The same insult devastates one person and amuses another. What explains the difference, on the Stoic account?","choices":["The words were actually different","One person has biologically thicker skin","Not the event but their judgments about it \u2014 harm comes from assent to the impression, and freedom lives in the gap between","Insults only hurt the weak"],"answer":2,"explain":"Impressions vs. assent: events don\u2019t harm us directly \u2014 our judgment about events does. Between the impression (the insult) and the reaction there\u2019s a gap, and in that gap, Epictetus says, lives freedom. Same words, different assents, different outcomes."}
],
"philosophy:6": [
{"q":"The capstone demands each principle be stated \u201cwith its reason, not just its slogan.\u201d Why?","choices":["Slogans are banned from the Academy","A slogan never examined is a shadow \u2014 the lesson-2 Socratic test requires knowing why you believe it","Longer essays get better marks","Reasons impress other people"],"answer":1,"explain":"An unexamined slogan is precisely the kind of secondhand projection the cave allegory warns about. \u2018What would change my mind?\u2019 is the question that separates a conviction from a catchphrase \u2014 and only convictions survive contact with reality."},
{"q":"\u201cA philosophy you can\u2019t find on a bad day isn\u2019t a philosophy \u2014 it\u2019s a souvenir.\u201d What does this demand of the one daily practice?","choices":["Practice only when you feel good","It must be small and robust enough to survive an ordinary bad day \u2014 the Aristotelian habit that keeps the philosophy alive","Philosophy is only for crises","Write it on expensive paper so you respect it"],"answer":1,"explain":"The daily practice is where the whole course cashes out: virtue as habit (lesson 4) means the philosophy lives in repetition, not in the one-page document. If the practice requires ideal conditions, it will be abandoned exactly when it\u2019s needed most."},
{"q":"The capstone has you revisit \u2014 and compare against \u2014 the three-sentence answer you dated in lesson 1. What is the point of that comparison?","choices":["To prove you did the work","To make your own change visible \u2014 philosophy as an examined, evolving position rather than fixed dogma","Handwriting practice","To pick a final answer and never revise it again"],"answer":1,"explain":"Dating the original turns your own mind into evidence: six lessons should have moved something, and seeing what moved \u2014 and what survived questioning \u2014 is the examined life in miniature. A philosophy that can\u2019t show its work is just a mood."}
]
});

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.quizzes, {
"gymnastics:1": [
{"q":"Why did the Greeks treat physical training as part of education rather than recreation?","choices":["They believed only athletes could be citizens","Because the person is a unity \u2014 a weak, restless body undermines attention, courage, and judgment, the very capacities the intellectual arts require","It was mainly religious ritual","They had no concept of leisure"],"answer":1,"explain":"Paideia \u2014 the formation of a person \u2014 had two wings: music for the soul, gymnastics for the body. Training wasn\u2019t a hobby alongside education; it was maintenance on the instrument you think with, and modern research on movement, cognition, and mood agrees with the gymnasium."},
{"q":"Aristotle\u2019s school was called the Peripatetics \u2014 \u201cthe walkers\u201d \u2014 because they taught while walking. What does the example illustrate?","choices":["Walking is superior to sitting in all cases","The Greeks integrated movement with thinking instead of separating them","Aristotle was always lost","Exercise can replace study"],"answer":1,"explain":"Philosophy done on foot is the Greek ideal in miniature: mind and body schooled together from childhood. The lesson\u2019s 10-minute walk isn\u2019t fitness advice \u2014 it\u2019s an experiment in noticing what movement does to thinking."},
{"q":"Juvenal\u2019s \u201cmens sana in corpore sano\u201d \u2014 a sound mind in a sound body \u2014 was originally a prayer. What does that framing add?","choices":["It was meant sarcastically","It presents the pairing as something to ask for and cultivate deliberately, not a guaranteed fact or a boast","It proves the Romans invented exercise","It\u2019s about the gods, not the body"],"answer":1,"explain":"A prayer is an aspiration plus an acknowledgment: this is what you should want, and it won\u2019t happen by accident. Read that way, the line is a program \u2014 train the body as part of forming the person \u2014 rather than a slogan for people who already have both."}
],
"gymnastics:2": [
{"q":"A beginner with a rounded, unstable hinge wants to start deadlifting heavy, arguing \u201cthe hinge protects the back.\u201d What is the flaw?","choices":["Deadlifts are inherently dangerous","The hinge protects the back only when the pattern is correct \u2014 loading a bad hinge trains injury, so pattern comes before load, always","Beginners cannot get strong","Hinges don\u2019t actually matter"],"answer":1,"explain":"The hinge is a spinal-safety skill \u2014 hips back, back flat, load on hips and hamstrings \u2014 and that safety exists only in the correct pattern. Adding weight to a collapsed hinge doesn\u2019t strengthen the back; it rehearses the injury. The course\u2019s first safety law: pattern first, load second."},
{"q":"You have no pull-up bar and no gym. Which option preserves the pull pattern the course requires?","choices":["Do extra push-ups instead","Table rows using a sturdy table edge, or a dead hang from anything solid overhead","Go for a run","Stretch your shoulders"],"answer":1,"explain":"The pull is the hardest pattern to train without equipment, but the pattern is what matters, not the apparatus. Table rows and dead hangs load the pulling muscles through the real movement \u2014 substituting push-ups would train a different pattern entirely and leave the pull unbuilt."},
{"q":"\u201cWalk while holding weight \u2014 grocery bags count.\u201d What principle does the carry example illustrate?","choices":["Shopping is a complete workout","Form doesn\u2019t matter for carries","Equipment is optional; the patterns are not \u2014 load is everywhere in daily life","You need a gym membership eventually"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Greek wrestlers built formidable bodies from the five patterns progressively loaded, with no equipment industry at all. The grocery-bag carry makes the point concrete: what\u2019s non-negotiable is the movement pattern \u2014 the implement is whatever\u2019s heavy and at hand."}
],
"gymnastics:3": [
{"q":"According to the lesson, how do you know you\u2019re walking at zone 2 intensity?","choices":["Your heart rate is at its maximum","You can talk in full sentences but slightly prefer not to \u2014 or you can breathe through your nose the whole time","You\u2019re gasping between words","You can sing comfortably"],"answer":1,"explain":"Zone 2 is the conversational-but-not-chatty pace: full sentences possible, slightly effortful. That\u2019s the intensity that builds the mitochondrial engine \u2014 easy enough to sustain for 30+ minutes, hard enough to drive aerobic adaptation."},
{"q":"\u201cIntensity without a base is like a roof without walls.\u201d What does the analogy claim?","choices":["Roofs are a bad idea","Hard efforts require the mitochondrial, capillary, and cardiac infrastructure built by easy aerobic work \u2014 without it you can\u2019t recover from or sustain intensity","Intensity is useless","Walking alone is sufficient forever"],"answer":1,"explain":"Easy aerobic work builds the infrastructure \u2014 mitochondria, capillaries, cardiac efficiency \u2014 that lets you absorb hard efforts and come back from them. Skip the base and the impressive roof collapses: injury, burnout, stalled progress. The ancients built the base by walking everywhere."},
{"q":"A friend walks 30 minutes daily with ease and wants to jump straight to sprinting. What does the lesson\u2019s rule prescribe?","choices":["Sprint immediately \u2014 the base is clearly built","Stop walking and only sprint","Aerobic base before intensity: add short intervals (one minute brisk, two minutes easy, repeated) rather than leaping to sprints","Sprinting is forbidden"],"answer":2,"explain":"\u2018When walking feels easy, add short intervals\u2019 \u2014 the progression is graded, not a cliff. Intervals bridge base and intensity safely; jumping to sprints on an unprepared chassis is exactly the roof-without-walls error the lesson warns against."}
],
"gymnastics:4": [
{"q":"Why move up a rung only when 3\u00d712 feels solid, rather than as soon as you can grind out one ugly rep of the next rung?","choices":["Twelve is a magic number","Strength is a skill grooved by perfect repetitions \u2014 ugly reps train compensation patterns and invite injury","Ugly reps never count toward anything","To make progress slower"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every rep is practice, and you get good at what you rehearse. Owning a rung (3\u00d712 clean) means the pattern is grooved; the next rung then builds on solid ground. One ugly rep of the harder variation mostly rehearses how to cheat it \u2014 the palaestra punished sloppy form for exactly this reason."},
{"q":"You can do 3\u00d78 incline push-ups cleanly but only 2 wobbly floor push-ups. Where should you train the push pattern?","choices":["Switch to floor push-ups now \u2014 harder is better","Skip to archer push-ups","Stay at incline until 3\u00d712 feels solid \u2014 own the rung first, then advance","Stop training push and focus on pull"],"answer":2,"explain":"The protocol: pick the hardest rung you can do for 3 sets of 8 clean reps, train it 2\u20133 times a week, and move up when 3\u00d712 feels solid. Two wobbly floor reps are a test result, not a training rung \u2014 training happens where form is clean."},
{"q":"\u201cClean reps only \u2014 the palaestra punished sloppy form, and so should you.\u201d What training principle does this express?","choices":["The Greeks were unnecessarily harsh","Progressive overload applies to quality movement \u2014 load and reps increase only on top of clean execution","Form is more important than getting stronger","Never increase difficulty"],"answer":1,"explain":"Progressive overload means demanding slightly more than last time \u2014 but \u2018more\u2019 has to mean more good reps, or the progression is fake. Clean execution is the currency; adding load to sloppy reps just buys injury at a higher price."}
],
"gymnastics:5": [
{"q":"Why is sleep called \u201cpart of training\u201d rather than just rest?","choices":["Sleep burns more calories than exercise","The adaptations training aims at \u2014 muscle repair, neural grooving of movement patterns, memory consolidation \u2014 physically occur during sleep","Dreams teach proper form","Rest is laziness"],"answer":1,"explain":"The workout is the stimulus; sleep and food are the adaptation. Growth hormone peaks, the nervous system resets, and your Logic and Grammar lessons consolidate \u2014 all overnight. Skipping sleep doesn\u2019t just rest less; it cancels part of the training\u2019s effect, measurably degrading strength, reaction time, and judgment."},
{"q":"An athlete trains hard, eats little protein, sleeps five erratic hours a night, and has stalled for two months. Per the lesson, what is the most likely limiter?","choices":["Not enough training volume","Recovery \u2014 the stimulus is there but adaptation is starved: 7\u20139 consistent hours of sleep and roughly a palm-sized portion of protein per meal","Genetics","Lack of supplements"],"answer":1,"explain":"\u2018You don\u2019t get stronger from training \u2014 you get stronger from recovering from training.\u2019 Before adding volume or buying powders, fix the two unglamorous pillars: consistent 7\u20139 hour sleep and enough protein and total fuel. The cheapest performance enhancer in existence is a fixed bedtime."},
{"q":"The Greek athlete\u2019s diet (figs, cheese, bread, meat) translates today to: enough protein, mostly whole foods, enough fuel. What principle does this express?","choices":["Ancient diets are nutritionally optimal","Simple and sufficient beats mystical and complicated \u2014 cover protein, eat mostly whole foods, fuel recovery","Eat only figs and cheese","Avoid all meat"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson\u2019s nutrition advice is deliberately unglamorous: a palm-sized portion of protein per meal, mostly whole foods, enough total fuel to recover \u2014 \u2018no mysticism required.\u2019 Recovery nutrition is a sufficiency problem, not a secrets problem."}
],
"gymnastics:6": [
{"q":"In the pentathlon\u2019s max push-up test, \u201cform breaks = set over.\u201d Why end the set instead of counting a few sloppy reps?","choices":["To keep the test short","The test measures good-form strength \u2014 counting sloppy reps measures something else and rewards bad patterns","The Greeks disliked push-ups","Fatigue is dangerous"],"answer":1,"explain":"A baseline is only useful if it measures the same thing every time. Chest-to-a-fist\u2019s-height, unbroken, clean reps measure pushing strength; sagging, half-range reps measure your willingness to cheat. The standard is what makes monthly retests comparable."},
{"q":"Why retest monthly instead of competing against others?","choices":["Competition is banned in the Academy","Others are probably fitter","The opponent is last month\u2019s you \u2014 the five events track your own adaptation over time","Monthly is an arbitrary choice"],"answer":2,"explain":"\u2018The Greeks competed against others; you compete against last month\u2019s you \u2014 which is the only opponent that matters.\u2019 Recorded scores with dates turn the pentathlon into a longitudinal experiment on one subject: your own progress in aerobic base, strength, endurance, grip, and mobility."},
{"q":"Which pentathlon event captures your aerobic base \u201cin one number\u201d?","choices":["Max good-form push-ups","Bodyweight squats in 2 minutes","The timed brisk 1-mile walk or run","Sit-and-reach"],"answer":2,"explain":"Each event isolates one capacity: the mile times the aerobic engine built in lesson 3, push-ups measure pushing strength, squats leg endurance, the hang/carry grip and posture, sit-and-reach mobility. Five numbers, five capacities \u2014 the complete athlete, the complete you."}
]
});

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.flashcards, {
"astronomy": [
{"front":"planetes","back":"Greek for \u201cwanderers\u201d \u2014 Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn: the five star-like objects that move against the fixed stars. Memory hook: planets are literally the wanderers of the sky."},
{"front":"Ecliptic","back":"The narrow highway across the sky traveled by the sun and moon; the twelve zodiac constellations sit along it. Practical rule: planets are always found near the ecliptic \u2014 never near the celestial pole."},
{"front":"Retrograde motion","back":"Planets occasionally appearing to loop backward before resuming forward course. Ptolemy explained it with epicycles; Copernicus explained it as Earth (faster, inner) lapping outer planets \u2014 a perspective effect, like a faster car seeming to slide backward."},
{"front":"Epicycle vs. deferent","back":"In Ptolemy\u2019s model the planet rides a small circle (epicycle) whose center rides a big circle (deferent) around Earth. Key insight: stack enough circles and you can reproduce any observed path \u2014 fitting data \u2260 discovering truth."},
{"front":"Kepler\u2019s three laws","back":"1) Ellipses: orbits are ellipses with the sun at one focus. 2) Equal areas in equal times: planets move faster near the sun. 3) Harmony: period\u00b2 \u221d distance\u00b3 \u2014 the farther the planet, the slower its year, in exact proportion."},
{"front":"Eratosthenes\u2019 measurement","back":"~240 BC: a stick and a well at Syene and Alexandria. The 7.2\u00b0 shadow-angle difference = 1/50 of Earth\u2019s circle \u2192 circumference \u2248 39,000 km (true: 40,075 km). Lesson: difference between two observations \u2014 not one measurement \u2014 unlocks the scale."},
{"front":"AU and light-year","back":"Astronomical unit \u2248 150 million km (Earth\u2013sun distance), the solar system\u2019s yardstick. Light-year \u2248 9.5 trillion km. Proxima Centauri: 4.24 light-years = 270,000 AU \u2014 interstellar distances dwarf the solar system."},
{"front":"Three evidences for the Big Bang (+ honest unknowns)","back":"Evidence: redshift proportional to distance, the cosmic microwave background (cooled afterglow, measurable everywhere), light-element abundances matching predictions. Unknowns: what dark matter/energy are, what caused the Bang, whether \u2018before\u2019 is meaningful."}
],
"philosophy": [
{"front":"Eudaimonia","back":"Usually \u2018happiness,\u2019 better \u2018flourishing\u2019 \u2014 the good life as an objective condition of a life going well, like health. Not a feeling: you can feel happy while your life goes badly; you cannot flourish while it does."},
{"front":"Elenchus","back":"Socratic cross-examination: take a confident claim, clarify its terms, follow the implications until confidence dissolves into a better question. The two starter questions: \u2018What do I actually mean by that?\u2019 and \u2018What would change my mind?\u2019"},
{"front":"The tripartite soul (Plato)","back":"Reason (the charioteer), spirit (the noble horse: ambition, anger), appetite (the unruly horse: hunger, lust). Justice in the soul = each part doing its job under reason\u2019s guidance. Maps to modern \u2018system 1 vs system 2\u2019 talk."},
{"front":"The allegory of the cave","back":"Prisoners chained facing a wall mistake shadows for reality; one escapes, sees the sun, returns to free the others \u2014 and is mocked. Diagnostic use: \u2018What shadows am I watching right now?\u2019 (reputation, outrage, status, feeds)."},
{"front":"Doctrine of the mean (Aristotle)","back":"Each virtue sits between two vices: courage between cowardice and recklessness, generosity between stinginess and wastefulness. Hitting the mean takes phronesis \u2014 practical wisdom \u2014 not a rulebook, because rules can\u2019t cover every case."},
{"front":"Virtue as habit (Aristotle)","back":"\u2018We become just by doing just acts, brave by doing brave acts.\u2019 Virtue is built by repetition like a muscle \u2014 not absorbed from lectures. Practice rule: design tiny daily acts and start embarrassingly small so the habit survives your worst day."},
{"front":"Dichotomy of control (Stoicism)","back":"Some things are up to us (judgments, intentions, responses); everything else is not. Suffering comes from demanding control over the second list. Two-column journal: act only on the left column."},
{"front":"Preferred indifferent (Stoicism)","back":"Health, wealth, reputation: rational to pursue (preferred) but indifferent to flourishing \u2014 they can\u2019t make you good or ruin you. Pursue energetically; refuse enslavement to outcomes. The four cardinal virtues: wisdom, justice, courage, temperance."}
],
"gymnastics": [
{"front":"Paideia\u2019s two wings","back":"Music (everything we call the humanities) for the soul + gymnastics for the body. A neglected body = an unfinished education. Training is maintenance on the instrument you think with \u2014 movement improves cognition, mood, sleep, stress resilience."},
{"front":"The five movement patterns","back":"Squat, hinge, push, pull, carry. Master them unloaded first. Equipment is optional; the patterns are not \u2014 the Greek wrestlers built formidable bodies from these alone, progressively loaded."},
{"front":"The hinge","back":"Hips push back, back flat \u2014 like closing a car door with your hips. The spinal-safety pattern behind all loaded lifting. Iron law: pattern first, load second \u2014 loading a bad hinge trains injury."},
{"front":"Zone 2","back":"Pace where you can talk in full sentences but slightly prefer not to (or breathe through your nose). Builds the mitochondrial engine that powers everything else. Rule: aerobic base before intensity \u2014 walk briskly 30+ min most days first."},
{"front":"Progressive overload","back":"Gradually demanding slightly more than last time. Bodyweight method: find the hardest rung you can do for 3\u00d78 clean reps, train 2\u20133\u00d7/week, advance when 3\u00d712 feels solid. Example ladder (push): wall \u2192 incline \u2192 knees \u2192 full \u2192 feet elevated \u2192 archer."},
{"front":"Clean reps only","back":"Advance rungs only on perfect execution. Strength is a skill grooved by perfect repetitions \u2014 ugly reps rehearse compensation and invite injury. The palaestra punished sloppy form; so should you."},
{"front":"Recovery = adaptation","back":"Training is the stimulus; sleep and food are the adaptation. Sleep 7\u20139 hours at consistent times \u2014 growth hormone peaks, memories consolidate, the nervous system resets. Food: palm-sized protein per meal, mostly whole foods, enough fuel. No mysticism."},
{"front":"The pentathlon baseline","back":"Five events, no equipment beyond a timer: timed 1-mile walk/run, max good-form push-ups, squats in 2 min, dead hang or farmer\u2019s carry, sit-and-reach. Record all five with the date; retest monthly; compete against last month\u2019s you."}
]
});

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.exams, {
"astronomy": [
{"q":"A student measures the sun\u2019s shadow angle at noon in her own city and announces she has computed Earth\u2019s circumference. What is missing from her method?","choices":["A longer stick","The comparison \u2014 a second location: one shadow angle alone is meaningless, only the difference between two locations unlocks the scale","A more precise clock","A deeper well"],"answer":1,"explain":"Eratosthenes\u2019 genius was differential: the 7.2\u00b0 difference between Syene and Alexandria is a fraction of Earth\u2019s full circle, and scaling the inter-city distance by that fraction gives the circumference. A single angle has no partner to differ from \u2014 it\u2019s a number in search of a meaning."},
{"q":"Suppose two rival models both predict planetary positions accurately. On the lesson\u2019s grounds, how should we choose between them?","choices":["Pick the older one \u2014 it has seniority","Not by prediction alone: prefer the one with the right mechanism and greater simplicity \u2014 Copernicus explains retrograde naturally, and predictive success is not the same as truth","Pick whichever uses more mathematics","Prediction is all that matters, so the choice is arbitrary"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ptolemy\u2019s 1,400-year predictive run proves that getting the numbers right doesn\u2019t mean getting the world right. The lesson\u2019s criterion is deeper: which model has the true mechanism, with the fewest contrivances? That\u2019s why Copernicus\u2019s simplicity counted even before the data forced the issue."},
{"q":"Using the basketball-sun scale model, a friend claims the planets are packed close together. Which detail of the model refutes this?","choices":["The sun is too small in the model","Earth is a peppercorn 26 meters from the basketball, and Neptune is a marble nearly a kilometer away \u2014 the solar system is overwhelmingly empty","Basketballs aren\u2019t to scale","The model leaves out the Moon"],"answer":1,"explain":"The model\u2019s whole point is to make emptiness felt in your legs: pace out 26 meters for a peppercorn Earth and nearly a kilometer for Neptune, and \u2018packed\u2019 becomes absurd. Then remember Proxima Centauri would be another basketball in Hawaii \u2014 the nothing between stars dwarfs the nothing between planets."},
{"q":"Planet A orbits closer to the sun than planet B. Which two things must be true of A relative to B?","choices":["A moves slower and has a longer year","A moves faster along its orbit and completes its year sooner","A is larger and hotter","A\u2019s orbit is more circular"],"answer":1,"explain":"Kepler\u2019s second law (equal areas in equal times) forces the nearer planet to move faster; the third law (period\u00b2 \u221d distance\u00b3) forces its year to be shorter. Closeness dictates both speed and period \u2014 two laws, one geometry."},
{"q":"Galaxy X shows greater redshift than galaxy Y. Assuming both redshifts are cosmological, what follows?","choices":["X is moving sideways faster","X is farther away \u2014 redshift grows with distance, which is the core evidence for expansion","X is older","Y is about to collide with us"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson\u2019s single strongest evidence is redshift proportional to distance: the farther the galaxy, the more stretched its light. That systematic pattern \u2014 in every direction \u2014 is what expansion predicts, and it\u2019s why redshift functions as a cosmic distance ladder."},
{"q":"What attitude does the lesson endorse toward dark matter and dark energy?","choices":["They are confirmed particles we fully understand","Treat them as named placeholders for observed effects \u2014 honest ignorance, in the Socratic spirit","Dismiss them as fiction","Assume they caused the Big Bang"],"answer":1,"explain":"We observe gravitational and expansion effects that visible matter can\u2019t explain, so we label the unknowns instead of feigning understanding. The lesson presents this as scientific virtue, not failure: \u2018I know that I know nothing\u2019 remains good practice 2,400 years later."},
{"q":"Why could ancient sailors navigate by Polaris but not by a brighter star like Sirius?","choices":["Polaris is the brightest star in the sky","Polaris sits nearly on Earth\u2019s rotation axis, so it stays put while the whole sky \u2014 Sirius included \u2014 wheels around it","Sirius moves too fast to track","Polaris is closer to Earth"],"answer":1,"explain":"Navigation needs a fixed reference, and fixity comes from geometry, not brightness (Sirius is actually brighter). A star on the spin axis has almost no circle to trace \u2014 so Polaris marks north all night while every other star marches across the sky."},
{"q":"A student proposes adding a third stacked circle to rescue a failing epicycle model. What caution from the Ptolemy lesson applies?","choices":["Three circles are always sufficient","With enough circles you can fit any data \u2014 fitting observations is not the same as discovering the true mechanism","Circles are forbidden in modern science","The student should add a fourth circle instead"],"answer":1,"explain":"The epicycle lesson is really about overfitting: sufficient complexity can mimic any observation while moving no closer to reality. The right response to a failing model isn\u2019t more knobs \u2014 it\u2019s asking whether the underlying mechanism is wrong, as Copernicus did."},
{"q":"The capstone\u2019s Aurelius exercise asks you to zoom out to cosmic scale, then zoom back in and ask what still matters. What is this training?","choices":["Astronomical calculation","Filtering real problems from imagined ones \u2014 testing which worries survive contact with a larger scale","Memorization of the Meditations","Proving human life is meaningless"],"answer":1,"explain":"Aurelius wrote that many disturbances \u2018lie entirely in your imagination\u2019 and promised \u2018vast space for yourself\u2019 to whoever embraces the universe in mind. The exercise operationalizes that: hold each worry up against the night sky and watch the scale-dependent ones dissolve, leaving what genuinely matters."},
{"q":"Rank these units from smallest to largest, and state Proxima Centauri\u2019s distance in the largest: kilometer, astronomical unit, light-year.","choices":["Kilometer < AU < light-year; Proxima is 4.24 light-years (270,000 AU) away","AU < kilometer < light-year; Proxima is 4.24 AU away","Light-year < AU < kilometer; Proxima is 270,000 km away","Kilometer < light-year < AU; Proxima is 150 million km away"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ladder climbs: kilometer (human scale) \u2192 AU \u2248 150 million km (solar-system yardstick) \u2192 light-year \u2248 9.5 trillion km (interstellar scale). Proxima at 4.24 light-years \u2014 270,000 AU \u2014 shows why each rung exists: the AU is already useless for stars, and kilometers are useless for everything beyond the Moon."}
],
"philosophy": [
{"q":"A journalist\u2019s reputation is destroyed by false accusations, but her honesty and judgment are untouched. Which school would say her life can still be fully good \u2014 and why?","choices":["The Epicureans \u2014 reputation was never pleasant anyway","The Stoics \u2014 reputation is a preferred indifferent; virtue, the only true good, is intact","The Skeptics \u2014 she should suspend judgment about whether she was accused","Aristotle \u2014 reputation is irrelevant to flourishing"],"answer":1,"explain":"For Stoics, health, wealth, and reputation are preferred indifferents: worth pursuing, but incapable of making or ruining a life. Only virtue \u2014 excellence of character \u2014 is good in the full sense, and no accusation can touch it. (Aristotle, by contrast, admits externals partly condition flourishing.)"},
{"q":"A friend is anxious about death and chases ever-finer luxuries to feel okay. Which school\u2019s prescription directly addresses both problems?","choices":["The Stoics \u2014 train yourself to want nothing","The Epicureans \u2014 free yourself from fear (of death, of the gods) and from pain, and find tranquility in simple pleasures rather than luxury","The Skeptics \u2014 suspend judgment about whether you exist","Plato \u2014 contemplate the Forms"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Epicurean diagnosis is precise: anxiety comes from fear (especially of death and divine punishment) and pain, and luxury is a treadmill that never delivers the promised peace. Their therapy targets the fears directly and recalibrates desire toward what actually satisfies \u2014 tranquil, pain-free life."},
{"q":"A politician declares, \u201cI know the people want this policy.\u201d What is the correct elenctic response?","choices":["\u201cWhat do you mean by \u2018the people\u2019? How do you know? What would change your mind?\u201d","\u201cYou\u2019re wrong.\u201d","\u201cLet\u2019s look at polling averages.\u201d","\u201cAll politicians lie.\u201d"],"answer":0,"explain":"The elenchus doesn\u2019t counter-assert \u2014 it cross-examines: clarify the terms (\u2018the people\u2019? which people, measured how?), demand the evidence, and ask what would falsify the claim. Socrates\u2019 discovery was that most confident claims dissolve at the second question \u2014 which is exactly when real thinking starts."},
{"q":"A student studies only for grades and status, understanding nothing deeply, yet feels driven and \u2018ambitious.\u2019 Give the Platonic diagnosis.","choices":["Her reason is ruling perfectly","Her soul is just \u2014 ambition is a virtue","Her soul is disordered: spirit (ambition, status) and appetite have seized the reins while reason \u2014 the charioteer \u2014 is sidelined","She has escaped the cave"],"answer":2,"explain":"In the tripartite soul, ambition belongs to spirit (the noble horse) \u2014 noble only when harnessed by reason toward worthy ends. Grades-and-status as the master makes spirit the charioteer and reason the passenger: motion without direction. Justice in the soul isn\u2019t feeling driven; it\u2019s each part doing its job under reason\u2019s guidance."},
{"q":"A man gives away his rent money to a stranger and calls it generosity. How does the doctrine of the mean classify this?","choices":["Generosity \u2014 giving is always virtuous","Wastefulness \u2014 the excess vice flanking generosity; phronesis would have calibrated the giving to his situation","Cowardice","Justice"],"answer":1,"explain":"Generosity sits between stinginess (deficiency) and wastefulness (excess) \u2014 and the mean isn\u2019t a fixed amount but what practical wisdom (phronesis) judges right here, now, for this person. Giving what you can\u2019t afford isn\u2019t hitting the mean; it\u2019s missing it on the far side."},
{"q":"Two students fail the same exam. One is crushed for weeks; the other studies the errors and improves. Same event, opposite outcomes. What explains the difference on the Stoic account?","choices":["The exam was graded differently","Their judgments about the failure differ \u2014 harm comes from assent to the impression, and freedom lives in the gap between impression and reaction","One is naturally resilient","Failure only hurts the weak"],"answer":1,"explain":"Impressions vs. assent: the failing grade is an impression; \u2018I\u2019m finished\u2019 versus \u2018here\u2019s data\u2019 are assents, and the suffering (or growth) follows the assent, not the grade. Epictetus\u2019s gap between impression and reaction is where both students stood \u2014 they just stepped in opposite directions."},
{"q":"Which of the following is a \u201cshadow\u201d in the cave-allegory sense?","choices":["A measurement you took yourself","A trending narrative about an event you\u2019ve never examined firsthand, which you now feel certain about","A conversation with an eyewitness","A book you read critically"],"answer":1,"explain":"Shadows are secondhand projections mistaken for reality \u2014 and certainty-about-the-unexamined is the signature symptom. The lesson\u2019s diagnostic question (\u2018what shadows am I watching right now?\u2019) targets exactly this: mediated outrage and narrative standing in for the thing itself."},
{"q":"A lottery winner is miserable a year later. What does the lesson\u2019s account of eudaimonia predict about this?","choices":["It\u2019s impossible \u2014 money guarantees happiness","It\u2019s unsurprising: the win changed feelings and externals, but flourishing is the objective condition of a life going well \u2014 like health, not like mood","It proves eudaimonia is unattainable","It proves Stoicism false"],"answer":1,"explain":"If eudaimonia were a feeling, a fortune should secure it \u2014 but feelings adapt and externals are hostage to fortune. Flourishing names how the life is actually going: relationships, character, purposeful activity. Money can support those or rot them; it can\u2019t substitute for them."},
{"q":"The Epicureans and the Skeptics both prize tranquility. Where do they disagree?","choices":["They don\u2019t \u2014 they\u2019re the same school","On its source: Epicureans reach tranquility through a positive doctrine (remove fear and pain, enjoy simple pleasures); Skeptics reach it by suspending judgment altogether","On whether tranquility is pleasant","On whether philosophy should be studied"],"answer":1,"explain":"Same destination, opposite routes: the Epicurean has a theory of what to want and what to stop fearing, while the Skeptic refuses theories and finds peace in the refusal itself. Spotting that two schools can share an aim while disagreeing on the path is exactly the \u2018hold them as live options\u2019 thinking the course demands."},
{"q":"The capstone requires every principle to be stated with its reason, not just its slogan. Two students submit: \u2018Be kind\u2019 and \u2018Be kind, because people\u2019s inner lives are as real as mine and cruelty corrodes the cruel.\u2019 Which survives the course\u2019s standards, and why?","choices":["The first \u2014 brevity is clarity","The second \u2014 an unexamined slogan is a shadow; the Socratic test demands knowing why you believe it, and reasons are what survive questioning","Both equally","Neither \u2014 principles shouldn\u2019t be written down"],"answer":1,"explain":"A slogan you can\u2019t defend is a cave shadow \u2014 borrowed, unexamined, fragile. The reasoned version has passed through the elenchus (what do I mean? what would change my mind?) and the Aristotelian test (can I act on this daily?). That\u2019s the difference between a conviction and a catchphrase \u2014 and only convictions hold on a bad day."}
],
"gymnastics": [
{"q":"A beginner\u2019s hinge shows a rounded back and the hips barely move. He wants to add a loaded backpack \u2018to get stronger faster.\u2019 What is the correct coaching call, and why?","choices":["Allow it \u2014 load builds the pattern","Refuse the load: pattern first, load second \u2014 a rounded-back hinge under weight trains spinal injury, not strength","Allow it but only for hinges, not squats","Tell him hinges don\u2019t matter"],"answer":1,"explain":"The hinge is a spinal-safety skill only when executed correctly \u2014 hips back, back flat, load on hips and hamstrings. Weight doesn\u2019t fix a bad pattern; it cements it under resistance. The course\u2019s first safety law is absolute: groove the unloaded pattern, then \u2014 only then \u2014 add load."},
{"q":"You can do 3\u00d710 wall push-ups and 3\u00d76 incline push-ups with clean form. Per the progression protocol, where do you train the push pattern?","choices":["Wall \u2014 it\u2019s the easiest","Incline \u2014 the hardest rung you can do for 3\u00d78 clean reps","Floor \u2014 always train the hardest variation","Archer \u2014 aim high"],"answer":1,"explain":"The protocol: train the hardest rung you can perform for 3 sets of 8 clean reps, 2\u20133 times weekly, and advance when 3\u00d712 feels solid. Incline at 3\u00d76 clean is one step from the 3\u00d78 standard \u2014 that\u2019s the productive rung. Wall is maintenance; floor would be ugly reps."},
{"q":"Two walkers: Ana chats effortlessly the whole way; Ben is gasping between words. Which one is in zone 2?","choices":["Ana","Ben","Neither \u2014 zone 2 is the pace where you can talk in full sentences but slightly prefer not to","Both"],"answer":2,"explain":"Zone 2 sits between the two: full sentences possible but slightly effortful (or nose-breathing sustainable). Ana is below the stimulus threshold \u2014 pleasant but not driving aerobic adaptation; Ben is above it, into intensity he hasn\u2019t earned a base for. Precision about intensity is the whole discipline of lesson 3."},
{"q":"Two lifters follow identical programs. One sleeps 8 consistent hours; the other sleeps 5 erratic hours. Who adapts better, and why?","choices":["The 5-hour sleeper \u2014 less sleep means more training time","The 8-hour sleeper \u2014 muscle repair, neural grooving, and memory consolidation physically occur during sleep, so the stimulus is converted to adaptation overnight","Neither \u2014 sleep doesn\u2019t affect strength","The 5-hour sleeper \u2014 toughness builds character"],"answer":1,"explain":"\u2018You don\u2019t get stronger from training \u2014 you get stronger from recovering from training.\u2019 Sleep is when growth hormone peaks and the nervous system resets; one bad night measurably degrades strength, reaction time, and judgment. The 5-hour lifter isn\u2019t training harder \u2014 they\u2019re canceling part of every session."},
{"q":"You have zero equipment and want to train the carry pattern. What is the correct prescription?","choices":["Skip carries until you can buy weights","Farmer\u2019s carry with two heavy grocery bags \u2014 grip, core, and posture in one ancient exercise","Carry one light bag to stay safe","Carries require a gym"],"answer":1,"explain":"The carry is a pattern, not an implement: walk while holding weight. Two heavy bags load the grip, trunk, and posture exactly as the pattern demands \u2014 \u2018equipment is optional; the patterns are not.\u2019 One light bag unbalances the load and misses the point."},
{"q":"A trainee can dead-hang 30 seconds and wants to jump to pull-up negatives. What does the progression ladder say?","choices":["Go ahead \u2014 ambition drives progress","Don\u2019t skip rungs: dead hang \u2192 table rows \u2192 low rows \u2192 pull-up negatives \u2192 pull-ups \u2014 own each rung (3\u00d712 solid) before advancing","Skip rows; they\u2019re too easy","Pull-ups are impossible without a gym"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each rung exists because the jump between non-adjacent rungs is too large to bridge with clean form. Table rows and low rows build the pulling strength and pattern that make negatives productive instead of injurious. Skipping rungs is the strength equivalent of the roof-without-walls error."},
{"q":"Why would the Greeks schedule physical training before a logic lesson rather than treating exercise as unrelated?","choices":["To tire students out so they\u2019d sit still","Because movement improves cognition, mood, sleep, and stress resilience \u2014 the very faculties logic depends on","Tradition, with no reason","To punish slow learners"],"answer":1,"explain":"Paideia treats the person as a unity: a restless, under-moved body degrades attention and judgment \u2014 the instruments of logic. Training first isn\u2019t scheduling trivia; it\u2019s preparing the instrument you think with, exactly as the Peripatetics philosophized on foot."},
{"q":"A runner does hard intervals three times a week, no easy mileage, and keeps getting injured. Diagnose this with the lesson\u2019s framework.","choices":["They need harder intervals","Roof without walls: intensity without an aerobic base \u2014 the mitochondrial and structural infrastructure that lets you absorb hard efforts was never built","They need better shoes","Running is inherently injurious"],"answer":1,"explain":"Intervals are the roof: impressive, load-bearing \u2014 but only on walls. Easy aerobic work builds mitochondria, capillaries, and cardiac efficiency plus the tissue tolerance for impact; without that base, each hard session is damage without the machinery to convert it to fitness. Walk 30+ minutes most days first, then earn the intervals."},
{"q":"Why does the pentathlon test exactly these five events \u2014 mile, push-ups, squats, hang/carry, sit-and-reach?","choices":["They were chosen at random","Each isolates one capacity: aerobic base, pushing strength, leg endurance, grip/posture, mobility \u2014 the complete athlete in five numbers","They\u2019re the easiest to measure","The ancient pentathlon used these exact events"],"answer":1,"explain":"The five events map to five independent physical capacities, so the battery profiles the whole person rather than one specialty. (The ancient pentathlon \u2014 running, jumping, discus, javelin, wrestling \u2014 tested completeness the same way; these are its no-equipment heirs.) Retested monthly, they show which capacities are actually adapting."},
{"q":"A trainee eats clean whole foods but tiny portions, trains hard, and has stalled for six weeks. What is the most likely nutritional limiter?","choices":["They need exotic superfoods","Insufficient total fuel and protein \u2014 roughly a palm-sized portion of protein per meal and enough total food to recover; the stimulus is fine but adaptation is starved","They should cut carbs","Clean eating is the problem"],"answer":1,"explain":"The lesson\u2019s nutrition rule is a sufficiency rule, not a purity rule: enough protein, mostly whole foods, enough total fuel. \u2018Clean but tiny\u2019 fails the third clause \u2014 recovery is construction work, and construction needs materials. No mysticism required; just enough food."}
]
});
